11	 * How to implement a new type of math operation:
12	 * 1. Create your new math operation class by deriving from this class. Put the new class in the namespace
13	 *      "Math" to avoid name collisions.
14	 * 2. Implement required virtual functions:
15	 *      a. outputType(DataType inputType):
16	 *          Indicates what kind of result your math operation creates, depending on the type of its input.
17	 *          If the input type is not applicable for your operation (e.g. frequency domain data as input for
18	 *          a (forward) DFT, you can return DataType::Invalid
19	 *      b. description():
20	 *          Return a short, user-readable description how the operation is set up. This will be displayed
21	 *          in the math edit dialog.
22	 *      c. edit():
23	 *          Optional. If your operation has customizable parameters, calling this function should start a
24	 *          dialog that allows the user to change these parameters.
25	 *      d. inputSamplesChanged(unsigned int begin, unsigned int end)
26	 *          This slot gets called whenever the input data has changed. Override it and implement your math
27	 *          operation in it. Parameters begin and end indicate which input samples have changed: If, for
28	 *          example, only the 2nd and third input values have changed, they are set like this: begin=1 end=3
29	 *          CAUTION: the size of the input vector may have changed, check before accessing it.
30	 *
31	 *          Emit the signal outputSamplesChanged(unsigned int begin, unsigned int end) after your operation is
32	 *          finished. Also call either success(), warning() or error() at the end of this slot, depending on
33	 *          whether the operation succeeded:
34	 *              success(): everything went well, output data contains valid values
35	 *              warning(): something might be wrong (e.g. not enough input samples to create meaningful data, ...).
36	 *                          Provide a hint by passing a short description string
37	 *              error(): something went wrong (called with wrong type of data, mathematical error, ...).
38	 *                          Provide a hint by passing a short description string
39	 *      e. getType(): return the type of the operation
40	 *      f. toJSON() and fromJSON(). Save/load all internal parameters
41	 * 3. Add a new type to the Type enum for your operation
42	 * 4. Extend the createMath(Type type) factory function to create an instance of your operation
